assertion  XSH.scalbn.001
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       scalbn(x, n) equals x times FLT_RADIX to the power n (computed by
           repeated multiplication or division, which is exact for these small
           exponents) for (1.5, 3), (1, -2), (-3, 2), (0.5, 1), (3, 0), (0.75,
           -4) and (2.5, 5).
test       t/001-scalbn-values.c

assertion  XSH.scalbn.002
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2001
says       scalbn(1, INT_MAX), scalbn(-1, INT_MAX) and scalbn(MAX, INT_MAX), with
           MAX the largest finite value of the type, overflow: the result is
           HUGE_VAL (negated for a negative x) and the range error is reported
           through errno (ERANGE) and the overflow exception as math_errhandling
           selects.
test       t/002-scalbn-overflow.c

assertion  XSH.scalbn.003
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       scalbn(1, INT_MIN) and scalbn(-1, INT_MIN) return a zero (the range
           error that may accompany it is not judged).
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/003-scalbn-underflow-zero.c

assertion  XSH.scalbn.004
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       A NaN argument gives a NaN result.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/004-scalbn-nan.c

assertion  XSH.scalbn.005
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       scalbn(+0, 5), (-0, 5), (+Inf, -3) and (-Inf, -3) return the first
           argument, with its sign.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/005-scalbn-zero-inf.c

assertion  XSH.scalbn.006
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       scalbn(x, 0) returns x for x equal to 3, -3, 0.1 and -0.1.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/006-scalbn-exponent-zero.c

assertion  XSH.scalbn.007
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       The smallest normal value of the type scaled by FLT_RADIX^-1 gives
           half of it (with a binary radix; the radix times smaller otherwise),
           exactly, when subnormal numbers exist.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard). A range error may accompany the result; it is not judged.
test       t/007-scalbn-subnormal.c

assertion  XSH.scalbn.008
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       With each rounding direction that can be selected (at least two are
           needed), scalbn(1.1, 3) and scalbn(-0.1, -2) equal 1.1*8 and -0.1/4,
           as written in the type of the call (a binary radix).
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard). The product with a power of two is exact in a binary
           radix; the test needs FLT_RADIX 2.
test       t/008-scalbn-rounding-mode.c

assertion  XSH.scalbn.009
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/scalbn.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.23
class      required
since      2001
says       With errno holding EDOM before the call, scalbn(1.5, 3) leaves it
           unchanged and, when exception flags are in use, raises none of the
           invalid, divide-by-zero and overflow exceptions.
test       t/009-scalbn-no-error.c

